      "title": "General Pants Co enters voluntary administration, putting hundreds of jobs at risk",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/19/general-pants-co-enters-voluntary-administration-jobs-at-risk",
      "description": "<p>Australian fashion chain becomes latest retailer in trouble after David Jones, Cue and Veronika Maine</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>The youth fashion chain General Pants Co has become the latest major retailer to slide into voluntary administration, putting nearly 500 jobs at risk as Australia’s retail sector continues to struggle with cash-strapped shoppers and online-only competitors.</p><p>The collapse comes not long after that of the upmarket <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/15/myer-david-jones-stores-closures-administration\">department store David Jones</a> and premium womenswear brands Cue and Veronika Maine, underscoring the mounting toll of tough economic conditions on established retailers.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/19/general-pants-co-enters-voluntary-administration-jobs-at-risk\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-19T04:10:10"
    },
    {
      "title": "Australia may have ‘one foot in recession’ as unemployment surges to five-year high",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/19/unemployment-rate-jumps-highest-level-five-years-job-figures",
      "description": "<p>Jobless rate rises from 4.7% to 4.9% in October, Bureau of Statistics figures reveal</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Australia may already have “one foot in recession” after unemployment surged to 4.9% in October, from 4.7%, in a shock increase that will add to rising worries that this year’s five interest rate hikes have pushed the economy past a tipping point.</p><p>New figures from the Australian Bureau of Statistics showed the number of employed people dropped by 43,500 in the month, confounding forecasts for jobs growth and a steady jobless rate.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/19/unemployment-rate-jumps-highest-level-five-years-job-figures\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-19T03:32:42"
    },
    {
      "title": "Beau Lamarre-Condon’s ‘wounded pride’ after breakup with Jesse Baird led to ‘darker plan for revenge’, crown alleges",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/19/beau-lamarre-condon-jesse-baird-luke-davies-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Prosecutor argues ‘stalking and break-in’ was ‘astonishing conduct for a serving police officer’ </p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Beau Lamarre-Condon’s “wounded pride” after his breakup with Jesse Baird drove him to a “far darker plan for revenge”, a court has heard.</p><p>The former police officer is accused of fatally shooting Baird and his new partner, Luke Davies, with his police service weapon in Baird’s Paddington home in Sydney on 19 February 2024.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/19/beau-lamarre-condon-jesse-baird-luke-davies-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-19T03:10:20"
    },
    {
      "title": "‘I’ll take the rap for that’: labour hire boss asked for fake invoices for sports club sponsorships, Big Build royal commission hears",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/19/cfmeu-big-build-inquiry-labour-hire-fake-invoices-sports-clubs-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Invoices, including for junior football and netball sponsorships totalling thousands of dollars, were part of ‘distorted’ payments to CFMEU official, inquiry told</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>A labour hire company distorted payments, including to a former top CFMEU official, by creating fake invoices for sports club sponsorships worth thousands of dollars.</p><p>BK Labour Hire director Bernard Kearney continued his evidence to the royal commission into alleged corruption across Victoria’s construction sector on Thursday.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/19/cfmeu-big-build-inquiry-labour-hire-fake-invoices-sports-clubs-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
